            <ReferenceHouse IncludeGeneration="false" IncludeHeatPump="false" HPLoadToType1="true" HPStandPerfLevel="false" NeutraliseFenestration="false" userHDD="0" areaAgPw="0" areaBgPw="0" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** The ductwork for the furnace is allowing air infiltration in the bathroom and the back bedroom, which would not happen if the ductwork was fully sealed without an air leakage path to the exterior of the house. It is likely coming through the belly bag/ crawl space area. No obvious pathway was identified from my investigation. It could be coming all the way from the front addition.



** Around wire penetrations and duct work penetrations through the envelope. These can be easily fixed with spray foam.



** Around the edge of some light fixtures. This can be sealed with caulking.



** The bathroom window is very leaky. Air is coming in around both the left and right side of the glass where the glass has a gap at the frame.



** Around any electrical outlet on an exterior wall. You can buy foam gaskets to put behind the face plate, which will help seal them up.



** Along the edges of the front addition door, facing north. This door is not sealing properly and could be due to the front addition shifting relative to the house. Ensure all weather stripping is installed correctly.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest, 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $50 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.8 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main walls of your home appear to be 2x6 construction with batt insulation (R20). I have modelled adding 2 additional inches (R10) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior (total R30). If you were to do an upgrade like this you would also need to extend out the eaves of your roof, making this an unlikely upgrade for your home. Due to the large amount of labour involved in adding insulation to the exterior of your home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 2 inches (R10) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 360 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15 GJ, and qualify for up to a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</mainWalls>
                <windows>WINDOWS



10 out of 13 windows in this house are vinyl framed, double pane, argon filled windows, with one low e coating. The remaining 3 windows are triple paned, argon filled, double low e windows (the two large livingroom windows, kitchen front). All of these windows have been upgraded relatively recently and are in good condition. Despite the fact that you are not likely to upgrade your windows I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading 10 out of 13 windows to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, the windows I modelled are, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 90 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.8 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

PROPANE Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric



You currently have a 170 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, instantaneous, condensing, gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $ 1290 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by 0.3 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

PROPANE FURNACE



Your house is currently heated with a new 80% efficient propane furnace. Just to show you the fuel savings difference I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 570 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



PELLET STOVE



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from your existing furnace and the cost of delivered pellets in Yellowknife at the $353.85/tonne if you were to install a pellet stove, used for about 25% of the heating requirement of the house. You can use your furnace fan to circulate some of the pellet heat to other portions of your home.



I modeled an upgrade to a new EPA qualified pellet stove that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe and an efficiency of at least 80%. Pellet stoves must meet either the Canadian Standards Association (CSA) B415.1-M92 (performance testing of solid-fuel burning appliances) or the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) wood burning appliances standards (40 CFR Part 60).



Rebates &amp; Savings: By using an 78% efficient pellet stove for 25% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house, you could potentially save $220 per year in energy costs, assuming you keep your existing 80% efficient furnace. Installing an eligible pellet stove qualifies for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------







</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

         

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Scott Dowler, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: scott.dowler@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
